
AI Company — Canonical Architecture

Status: CanonicalRepository: /mnt/e/AI_Company1Architecture Role: Constitutional ReferenceImplementation Status: FoundationVersion: 1.0

1. Purpose

AI Company is a local-first autonomous software organization.

Its purpose is to transform company objectives into controlled,observable, resource-aware execution.

AI Company is not merely a collection of AI agents.

It is an organizational system composed of:

objectives

decisions

tasks

agents

capabilities

physical resources

execution

memory

experience

trust

learning

workflow coordination

The system must remain coherent even when individual agents,external services, models, or workflows change.

The architecture therefore distinguishes between:

company meaning

company decisions

executable work

execution mechanisms

external infrastructure

No external tool is allowed to become the owner of AI Company meaning.

2. Constitutional Principle

The central architectural principle is:

AI Company owns its own semantics.

External systems may provide:

intelligence

workflow execution

transport

orchestration

storage

communication

computation

but they do not define:

what a company objective means

what a task means

what an agent means

what a capability means

what successful execution means

what trust means

what experience means

what the canonical company state is

These concepts belong to AI Company.

3. Architectural Reality

AI Company exists simultaneously in several realities.

Company Intent
      |
      v
Decision Reality
      |
      v
Work Reality
      |
      v
Execution Reality
      |
      v
Physical Reality

The system must never reason as if execution were unlimited.

Every company decision eventually encounters physical constraints.

Those constraints include:

CPU

RAM

GPU

storage

network

execution availability

agent availability

external service availability

Therefore:

AI Company is resource-aware by architecture, not by optional optimization.

4. Canonical System Model

The canonical high-level architecture is:

                        HUMAN OWNER
                             |
                             v
                     COMPANY OBJECTIVES
                             |
                             v
                     STRATEGIC DECISION
                             |
                             v
                         TASK SYSTEM
                             |
                             v
                    EXECUTION DECISION
                             |
              +--------------+--------------+
              |                             |
              v                             v
         AGENT SYSTEM                 RESOURCE SYSTEM
              |                             |
              +--------------+--------------+
                             |
                             v
                     EXECUTION READINESS
                             |
                             v
                      EXECUTION SYSTEM
                             |
                             v
                     OBSERVATION / RESULT
                             |
              +--------------+--------------+
              |                             |
              v                             v
            MEMORY                       LEARNING
              |                             |
              +--------------+--------------+
                             |
                             v
                            TRUST

This model defines direction of responsibility.

It does not imply that every operation is synchronous.

5. The Four Canonical Layers

AI Company is divided into four primary architectural layers.

02_core
    |
    | defines company meaning
    v
03_runtime
    |
    | composes and operates the company
    v
04_integrations
    |
    | connects external systems
    v
06_tools

Tests and documentation exist outside the production dependency path.

6. Core Layer

The Core owns canonical domain meaning.

The Core contains the stable concepts that define AI Company.

These include:

Company Objective

Strategic Priority

Task

Agent

Capability

Resource Requirement

Resource State

Execution Readiness

Execution Result

Memory Record

Experience Record

Trust Record

The Core must not depend on:

Weft

LLM providers

HTTP clients

databases

external workflow engines

external agent platforms

The Core must remain meaningful if every integration is removed.

7. Runtime Layer

The Runtime composes canonical Core components.

The Runtime owns:

lifecycle composition

dependency construction

runtime services

orchestration boundaries

execution coordination

company operation

The Runtime may depend on Core.

Core may never depend on Runtime.

8. Integration Layer

Integrations connect AI Company to external systems.

Examples include:

Weft

LLM providers

external agents

databases

file systems

communication systems

web services

Integrations translate between:

External Contract
        |
        v
AI Company Canonical Contract

An integration must not redefine canonical domain models.

9. Tools Layer

Tools contain utilities used for:

diagnostics

repository inspection

development support

reporting

migration support

Tools do not own company semantics.

Production Runtime must not depend on development tools.

10. Canonical Dependency Direction

Dependencies must move inward.

Tools
   |
Integrations
   |
Runtime
   |
Core

Allowed:

Runtime -> Core
Integration -> Core
Integration -> Runtime contracts where explicitly defined
Tools -> all layers for inspection

Forbidden:

Core -> Runtime
Core -> Integration
Core -> Tools

Runtime -> Tools

No dependency may reverse architectural ownership.

11. Company Objective

A Company Objective represents desired organizational intent.

An objective is not executable by itself.

An objective may produce:

strategic decisions

projects

tasks

resource requirements

The Objective layer owns intent.

It does not own execution.

12. Strategic Decision

A Strategic Decision transforms objectives into organizational direction.

It determines:

what should be pursued

relative importance

strategic priority

expected value

constraints

Strategic decisions do not execute work.

13. Canonical Task

A Task is the canonical unit of company work.

There must be exactly one canonical company Task model.

A Task must not have competing parallel definitions in differentexecution subsystems.

The canonical task represents:

Identity
+
Intent
+
Requirements
+
Constraints
+
Lifecycle State
+
Execution Context
+
Result

A Task may be translated into an external execution contract,but the external contract is not a second canonical Task.

14. Task Ownership

Task lifecycle ownership must be explicit.

The canonical task lifecycle authority owns:

state transitions

transition validation

terminal states

No queue, pipeline, agent, integration, or workflow engine may mutatecanonical task state directly.

All transitions must pass through the canonical lifecycle authority.

15. Task Queue

A Task Queue owns pending work selection.

It may:

accept canonical tasks

order canonical tasks

return eligible tasks

It must not:

redefine tasks

execute tasks

mutate task lifecycle state

make strategic decisions

16. Agent

An Agent represents an available execution capability.

An Agent is not a Task.

An Agent may be:

local

external

AI-based

software-based

human

hybrid

The canonical Agent model describes:

identity

capabilities

availability

requirements

experience

trust context

Execution implementation details belong outside the canonicalAgent identity.

17. Capability

A Capability describes what can potentially be performed.

Capability is not execution.

Capability availability may depend on:

agents

hardware

software

external services

The Capability System describes availability.

It does not:

execute tasks

schedule tasks

make strategic decisions

18. Physical Hardware

AI Company operates inside finite physical reality.

Hardware is explicitly modeled.

Hardware observation includes, where available:

CPU capacity

CPU utilization

RAM total

RAM available

GPU presence

GPU capacity

storage capacity

storage availability

operating system information

Hardware observation is descriptive.

It does not make execution decisions.

19. Resource State

Resource State represents the currently usable execution capacity.

Resource State is derived from physical observation.

Examples include:

Hardware Observation
        |
        v
Resource State
        |
        v
Execution Readiness

Resource State may include:

available CPU

available RAM

available GPU

available storage

current system pressure

Resource State does not select agents.

20. Execution Readiness

Execution Readiness determines whether a proposed execution iscurrently possible.

It combines:

task requirements

agent requirements

capability requirements

resource state

availability constraints

The output is a readiness decision.

Examples:

READY

DEGRADED

BLOCKED

UNAVAILABLE

Readiness is not strategy.

Readiness answers:

Can this execution happen now under current conditions?

21. Decision System

The Decision System determines what should happen.

It operates on:

objectives

strategic priorities

tasks

capabilities

experience

trust

resources

Decision and execution remain separate.

Decision
   |
   v
Execution Authorization
   |
   v
Execution

A successful decision does not imply that execution is currently ready.

22. Agent Selection

Agent Selection determines the most appropriate agent for a task.

Selection may consider:

capability fit

experience

trust

availability

hardware compatibility

execution cost

risk

Agent selection does not execute the task.

Selection produces a selection decision.

23. Execution System

The Execution System performs authorized work.

Its responsibilities include:

receiving executable work

receiving an execution decision

invoking the selected executor

collecting results

reporting execution outcome

The Execution System must not:

redefine strategic priorities

redefine canonical tasks

redefine trust

redefine memory

24. Execution Pipeline

An Execution Pipeline defines ordered execution stages.

Pipeline stages may perform:

analysis

planning

generation

validation

application

testing

A Pipeline performs operations.

The Pipeline does not own canonical task lifecycle transitions.

Pipeline outcomes are reported to the canonical lifecycle authority.

25. Weft — The Nervous System

Weft is an external operational nervous system.

Weft may provide:

workflow transport

distributed orchestration

node execution

triggers

asynchronous coordination

workflow visibility

Weft does not own:

company identity

company objectives

canonical tasks

canonical agents

canonical trust

canonical experience

strategic decisions

The relationship is:

AI COMPANY
    |
    | canonical execution intent
    v
WEFT
    |
    | workflow transport / coordination
    v
EXECUTION

AI Company must remain conceptually valid without Weft.

Weft is replaceable infrastructure.

26. Memory

Memory stores organizational knowledge.

Memory may contain:

observations

execution history

outcomes

decisions

contextual knowledge

Memory does not itself decide.

Memory provides evidence to decision systems.

27. Experience

Experience represents structured learning from previous execution.

Experience may influence:

agent selection

task evaluation

planning

future execution decisions

Experience is evidence.

Experience is not execution authority.

28. Trust

Trust represents confidence derived from evidence.

Trust may be influenced by:

successful execution

failed execution

historical reliability

experience

observed trends

Trust must not be manually mutated by arbitrary execution components.

Trust updates must pass through a canonical trust authority.

29. Learning

Learning transforms outcomes into improved organizational knowledge.

The canonical direction is:

Execution
    |
    v
Observation
    |
    v
Memory
    |
    v
Experience
    |
    v
Trust / Learning Signals
    |
    v
Future Decisions

Learning does not bypass canonical decision boundaries.

30. Canonical Execution Flow

The canonical execution flow is:

Company Objective
        |
        v
Strategic Decision
        |
        v
Canonical Task
        |
        v
Task Evaluation
        |
        v
Agent Selection
        |
        v
Resource Evaluation
        |
        v
Execution Readiness
        |
        v
Execution Authorization
        |
        v
Execution System
        |
        v
Execution Pipeline
        |
        v
Execution Result
        |
        v
Canonical Task Lifecycle Update
        |
        v
Memory / Experience / Trust

No component may silently bypass this chain.

31. Composition Root

The Composition Root is the single location where productiondependencies are constructed and connected.

The Composition Root owns:

concrete dependency construction

integration selection

runtime configuration

system startup

Domain models must not construct integrations directly.

Core components must receive dependencies through explicit contracts.

32. Single Source of Truth Rule

Every canonical concept must have one authoritative definition.

Examples:

Concept                     Canonical Authority
------------------------------------------------
Task                        Canonical Task Model
Task State                  Task Lifecycle System
Agent                       Canonical Agent Model
Capability                  Capability System
Hardware Observation        Hardware Observer
Resource State              Resource State System
Execution Readiness         Readiness System
Trust                       Trust System
Experience                  Experience System
Company Runtime             Composition Root

Parallel models require explicit architectural justification.

33. Adapter Rule

External or legacy representations may exist only as adapters.

External Model
      |
      v
Adapter
      |
      v
Canonical Model

The adapter must not create a competing domain authority.

34. No Hidden Ownership

Every mutable state must have an identified owner.

Examples:

Task State
    -> Task Lifecycle Authority

Trust
    -> Trust Authority

Resource State
    -> Resource State System

Execution Queue
    -> Queue

Runtime Construction
    -> Composition Root

If ownership cannot be identified, the architecture is incomplete.

35. No Parallel Canonical Paths

AI Company must not maintain two competing production paths for thesame responsibility.

Examples of forbidden parallelism:

Two canonical task models

Two task lifecycle systems

Two worker execution paths

Two agent selection authorities

Two runtime composition roots

Alternative implementations are allowed only behind one canonicalcontract.

36. Local-First Principle

AI Company is local-first.

The canonical company state must not require permanent cloudavailability.

External services may amplify the system.

They must not be the sole owner of company identity or fundamentalcompany state.

The system must degrade explicitly when external dependencies areunavailable.

37. Finite Resource Principle

No task is assumed executable merely because it is desirable.

Execution requires physical feasibility.

Desired Work
      +
Available Capability
      +
Available Resources
      +
Execution Readiness
      =
Possible Execution

A strategically valuable task may remain blocked.

Blocked execution is a valid organizational state.

38. External Agent Principle

External agents are integrations.

They are represented internally through canonical Agent contracts.

An external provider must not redefine:

agent identity

task identity

trust

experience

Provider-specific representations remain inside integration adapters.

39. Human Personnel Principle

Human personnel can participate as Agents.

The architecture treats human and software execution through commonorganizational concepts while allowing different execution mechanisms.

A human Agent is not forced into an AI-specific implementation model.

40. Observability Principle

Every important organizational transition should be observable.

Important observations include:

decisions

task transitions

agent selection

readiness failures

execution outcomes

resource constraints

Observability records what happened.

Observability does not own the underlying state.

41. Failure Principle

Failure is a first-class system outcome.

The system must distinguish between:

unavailable

blocked

failed

degraded

successful

Failure must produce information that can later influence:

memory

experience

trust

learning

42. Architectural Invariants

The following invariants are mandatory.

Core has no dependency on integrations.

Every canonical concept has one authoritative model.

Every mutable state has an identified owner.

Tasks have one canonical lifecycle authority.

Agent selection is separate from execution.

Decision is separate from execution.

Resource observation is separate from resource decision.

Weft is infrastructure, not company identity.

External agents are integrations behind canonical contracts.

Hardware limitations are explicit architectural inputs.

Learning consumes outcomes; it does not directly execute work.

Memory provides evidence; it does not automatically decide.

Experience provides evidence; it does not automatically execute.

Trust has a canonical update authority.

Runtime composition occurs through one Composition Root.

Alternative implementations may exist behind canonical interfaces,but must not create competing canonical ownership.

43. Architectural Change Rule

This document is the canonical architectural reference.

Changes require explicit reasoning.

Before changing the architecture, the following questions must beanswered:

1. Which canonical responsibility changes?

2. Which ownership boundary changes?

3. Which dependency direction changes?

4. Does the change create a parallel authority?

5. Does the change violate an architectural invariant?

6. Can the same goal be achieved without changing the architecture?

Implementation convenience alone is not sufficient reason to changethe architecture.

44. Implementation Rule

Before creating a production module, the implementation must define:

Responsibility
Canonical Owner
Inputs
Outputs
Dependencies
Forbidden Dependencies
Mutable State
State Owner

No production module should be created before these answers exist.

45. Definition of Architectural Success

AI Company is architecturally successful when:

new functionality has an obvious ownership location

no competing canonical model exists

dependency direction remains predictable

external systems remain replaceable

physical resource limitations influence execution explicitly

decisions remain separate from execution

learning remains grounded in observed outcomes

the system can evolve without semantic fragmentation

46. Final Constitutional Rule

Implementation serves architecture.

Architecture must not be continuously rewritten to justifyimplementation shortcuts.

When code conflicts with this document:

Architecture
    wins.

The implementation must be corrected unless an explicit,well-justified architectural change is approved.

END OF CANONICAL ARCHITECTURE

